00:01:40.100 --> 00:01:40.500
RETO MEIER: Excellent.

00:01:40.500 --> 00:01:42.625
Well, I mean, I'm a huge
supporter of anything that

00:01:42.625 --> 00:01:44.840
involves this amount of pizza.

00:01:44.840 --> 00:01:46.160
But tell me.

00:01:46.160 --> 00:01:47.480
What is the point of this?

00:01:47.480 --> 00:01:48.830
Why is this in the Dev Zone?

00:01:48.830 --> 00:01:49.690
Why have we got this here?

00:01:49.690 --> 00:01:51.857
TERRY RYAN: So this uses
under the covers ML, right?

00:01:51.857 --> 00:01:52.580
Machine Learning.

00:01:52.580 --> 00:01:54.530
And ML is really
daunting, right?

00:01:54.530 --> 00:01:56.030
A lot of people
come to it, and they

00:01:56.030 --> 00:01:59.130
think they need to be a
data scientist to do it.

00:01:59.130 --> 00:02:02.780
And your rank and file developer
sometimes can't approach it.

00:02:02.780 --> 00:02:07.600
Well, we took kind of an
incredibly trivial use of ML

00:02:07.600 --> 00:02:09.350
and trained it to
determine whether or not

00:02:09.350 --> 00:02:11.030
pizza was authentic.

00:02:11.030 --> 00:02:13.550
And all of this, we did.

00:02:13.550 --> 00:02:14.690
We grabbed the data.

00:02:14.690 --> 00:02:15.960
We classified all the images.

00:02:15.960 --> 00:02:19.980
It was all done by me, who is an
app dev, not a data scientist.

00:02:19.980 --> 00:02:22.160
So it really shows
that anyone can do ML.

00:02:22.160 --> 00:02:23.995
If I can do it,
anyone can do it.

00:02:23.995 --> 00:02:26.370
RETO MEIER: So you could take
the same sort of technology

00:02:26.370 --> 00:02:27.745
and same approach
here and really

00:02:27.745 --> 00:02:29.900
apply it to much more
meaningful projects.

00:02:29.900 --> 00:02:30.650
TERRY RYAN: Right.

00:02:30.650 --> 00:02:33.463
If you wanted to take
satellite pictures of forests

00:02:33.463 --> 00:02:34.880
